12. FIREBASE DATA ARCHITECTURE
==================================================

Use Cloud Firestore.

All private data must be scoped by Firebase authenticated UID.

Suggested structure:

/users/{uid}
    /profile/main
    /products/{productId}
    /stockMovements/{movementId}
    /stockCalculations/{calculationId}
    /packagingCalculations/{calculationId}
    /privateEvents/{eventId}
    /savedPromotions/{promotionId}

Optional future collection:

/publicEvents/{eventId}

The publicEvents collection is read-only for ordinary users.

Every private query must reference the current authenticated UID.

Do not query all users' records.

Use server timestamps where appropriate.

Use safe error handling for failed reads and writes.

Do not store Gemini API keys in Firestore.

Do not store secrets in user profile documents.

Do not use a public Firestore security rule.

FIRESTORE SECURITY REQUIREMENTS

Generate and verify Firestore Security Rules.

Private user records:
- Only the authenticated owner can read, create, update, and delete their own records.
- UID in the path must match request.auth.uid.
- Unauthenticated access is denied.

Public events:
- Public read access only if a publicEvents collection is implemented.
- Ordinary users cannot create, edit, or delete public events.

Default:
- Deny all other access.

Also validate important field types and reasonable field limits where practical.

Never publish with insecure rules.

